    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "50-59",
      "detail": "5th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "A flat season keeps refusing to die, and Tuesday's wobble gave way to a cleaner Wednesday - <strong>the Blue Jays beat the Nationals 5-2</strong> behind Trey Yesavage's strongest outing yet, six and two-thirds innings of one-run ball. That's the kind of start a club drags around like a lifeboat when it sits at 50-59, fifth in the AL East, 14 games back and still chasing respect more than relevance. Kazuma Okamoto's three-hit, two-RBI night was a welcome jolt; that's a bat worth watching in a lineup that has too often gone quiet. Toronto has now won two of its last three after the 8-6 loss and the 3-2 win in Washington framed a 3-3 trip. <strong>Next comes St. Louis on Friday night at 7:07 p.m. ET, with Yesavage's rotation case and the club's faint momentum both on trial.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 30, 2026"
      },
